  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates different ways metal species can exist on or in a porous solid support. The gray repeating framework represents the support, while the colored spheres indicate particle size and location. Blue dots are isolated atoms or very small clusters, red groups are nanoclusters, and larger green aggregates are nanoparticles.\n\nThe main message is a comparison of three states: “engaged,” “supported,” and “embedded.” In the engaged case, very small species occupy or interact closely with the pores or internal surface of the support. In the supported case, larger particles sit on the outer surface or at pore openings, appearing attached but still exposed. In the embedded case, the largest nanoparticles are partly buried within the support matrix, suggesting stronger confinement and more intimate incorporation into the material.\n\nOverall, the image emphasizes how particle size increases from single atoms to nanoclusters to nanoparticles, and how this size influences where the species can reside relative to the porous framework. Smaller species fit into internal spaces more easily, whereas larger nanoparticles are shown either resting on the surface or becoming embedded within the support structure.",
